Implement a byte-exact Rust port of the C++ program /workspace/dataset/test19.cpp.
Read that .cpp file first. Then build a complete Cargo project in /output.


GROUND TRUTH ALREADY ESTABLISHED (do not re-derive, trust these):
- The C++ binary is /workspace/dataset/test19_executable. Its output is INVARIANT to argv (verified with
  "", "0", "1", "-1", "abc", "1 2 3", "--help" -> identical md5). main() ignores argc/argv entirely.
- Spinner charset extracted from the binary's string table, in this exact order (8 braille glyphs, by codepoint):
    U+28FE U+28FD U+28FB U+283F U+287F U+28DF U+28EF U+28F7
  Frame index = next_count % 8.
  Evidence: CyclicProgress(5) after 3 next() -> frame 3; (10) after 6 -> frame 6; (15) after 9 -> frame 1 (9%8).
- ProgressDisplay format literal fragments found in the binary: "Progress: ", "% (", "/", ")".
- Get the exact expected bytes by RUNNING the binary and piping through od -c. Never hand-copy glyphs.

The C++ source of the program under port is /workspace/dataset/test19.cpp (READ IT - it is the entry
program, not the library). The library header ../include/indicators.hpp DOES NOT EXIST on disk and must
NOT be sought out; behavior is inferred black-box.


=== COMPONENT SPECS (derived by independent reverse-engineering agents) ===
{
  "component": "indicators::CyclicProgress (from /workspace/dataset/test19.cpp, binary /workspace/dataset/test19_executable)",
  "summary": "CyclicProgress is a 32-byte object holding `int count_` at +0x00 (init 0), `int total_` at +0x04 (ctor arg), and a `std::vector<std::string> frames_` at +0x08 holding 8 braille spinner glyphs (old COW libstdc++ ABI: element stride 8 bytes, `sar $0x3` in str()).\n\nCRITICAL CORRECTION #1 to the supplied ground truth: `next()` is NOT a plain increment. It is `count_ = (count_ + 1) % total_;` — a SIGNED int modulo. Disassembly of main (next() is inlined) at 0x4022b8: `add $0x1,%eax; cltd; idiv %ecx; ... mov %edx,0x30(%rsp)` with no cmp/cmov (no clamping). Consequence: for total>0 the count cycles 1,2,...,total-1,0,1,... and can never reach or exceed total, so the percentage never reaches 100.0%. The test happens not to wrap (3<5, 6<10, 9<15) so plain-increment and modulo agree there.\n\nCRITICAL CORRECTION #2: the charset's index 3 is U+28BF (⢿ BRAILLE PATTERN DOTS-1234568), NOT U+283F. Extracted from the frame table at vaddr 0x404047 (file offset 16455), 8 entries of 3 bytes + NUL, referenced one-by-one by the ctor at 0x4038d0 via _S_construct(0x404047,0x40404a), (0x40404b,0x40404e), ... :\n  idx0 E2 A3 BE U+28FE ⣾ | idx1 E2 A3 BD U+28FD ⣽ | idx2 E2 A3 BB U+28FB ⣻ | idx3 E2 A2 BF U+28BF ⢿\n  idx4 E2 A1 BF U+287F ⡿ | idx5 E2 A3 9F U+28DF ⣟ | idx6 E2 A3 AF U+28EF ⣯ | idx7 E2 A3 B7 U+28F7 ⣷\nU+283F's UTF-8 (E2 A0 BF) does not appear anywhere in the binary (grep -aob returned nothing); E2 A2 BF appears at file offset 16467 = 16455 + 3*4, i.e. exactly slot 3.\n\nstr() (0x402ac0) builds an ostringstream and inserts exactly four pieces:\n  1. frames_[ count_ % frames_.size() ] — 0x402bb4 `movslq (%rbx),%rax; xor %edx,%edx; div %rcx` : the int count_ is sign-extended then divided UNSIGNED, i.e. C++ `count_ % frames_.size()` with size_t conversion. Index = ((count as i64) as u64) % 8.\n  2. a single ASCII space 0x20 — `mov $0x1,%edx; mov $0x40403d,%esi` (1 byte; 0x40403d is the trailing space of the merged literal \"Progress: \").\n  3. the percentage: `cvtsi2ssl (%rbx),%xmm0` (count→f32), `cvtsi2ssl 0x4(%rbx),%xmm1` (total→f32), `divss %xmm1,%xmm0`, `mulss 0x404068(%rip),%xmm0` where [0x404068] = 00 00 C8 42 = 100.0f, then `cvtss2sd` and `_M_insert<double>`. Arithmetic is SINGLE precision, only widened to double for printing. Stream state set immediately before: `movq $0x1,0x8(%rdx)` (precision = 1) and `and $0xfffffefb,%eax; or $0x4,%eax` (clear floatfield 0x104 = fixed|scientific, set fixed 0x4) == `std::fixed << std::setprecision(1)`.\n  4. a single '%' — `mov $0x1,%edx; mov $0x404032,%esi`.\nEquivalent printf: \"%s %.1f%%\".\n\nVERIFICATION: I wrote a reference C++ implementation with exactly this state/next()/str() (/tmp/rev/ref.cpp), built with g++ -O2, and its output is byte-identical to `test19_executable | head -3` (cmp reports IDENTICAL: 342 242 277 ' ' 6 0 . 0 % \\n / 342 243 257 ... / 342 243 275 ...). Its str() disassembly is also instruction-for-instruction and stack-offset-for-stack-offset the same as the original's. Decoding the observed bytes: line1 E2 A2 BF = U+28BF = idx 3 = 3%8 (count 3, total 5); line2 E2 A3 AF = U+28EF = idx 6 (count 6, total 10); line3 E2 A3 BD = U+28FD = idx 1 = 9%8 (count 9, total 15). All three print 60.0% because 3/5 = 6/10 = 9/15.",
  "rust_signature_plan": "// Exact glyph bytes verified against the binary's table at vaddr 0x404047.\n// idx3 is U+28BF (E2 A2 BF), *not* U+283F.\nconst FRAMES: [&str; 8] = [\n    \"\\u{28FE}\", \"\\u{28FD}\", \"\\u{28FB}\", \"\\u{28BF}\",\n    \"\\u{287F}\", \"\\u{28DF}\", \"\\u{28EF}\", \"\\u{28F7}\",\n];\n\npub struct CyclicProgress {\n    count: i32, // C++ offset +0x00, initialized to 0\n    total: i32, // C++ offset +0x04, ctor argument\n    // frames_ (std::vector<std::string> at +0x08) is an implementation\n    // detail; a &'static [&str; 8] is behaviorally equivalent.\n}\n\nimpl CyclicProgress {\n    /// C++: CyclicProgress(int total) : count_(0), total_(total), frames_{...}\n    pub fn new(total: i32) -> Self {\n        Self { count: 0, total }\n    }\n\n    /// C++: void next() { count_ = (count_ + 1) % total_; }\n    /// Signed, truncating modulo. Panics when total == 0, mirroring the\n    /// C++ `idiv` SIGFPE. wrapping_add mirrors the practical result of the\n    /// (technically UB) int overflow at count == i32::MAX.\n    pub fn next(&mut self) {\n        self.count = self.count.wrapping_add(1) % self.total;\n    }\n\n    /// C++: frames_[count_ % frames_.size()]\n    /// int -> size_t conversion, hence sign-extend then unsigned modulo.\n    fn frame(&self) -> &'static str {\n        FRAMES[((self.count as i64 as u64) % FRAMES.len() as u64) as usize]\n    }\n\n    /// C++: static_cast<float>(count_) / total_ * 100.0f\n    /// MUST be f32 throughout (divss/mulss); f64 gives different output.\n    fn percent_f32(&self) -> f32 {\n        self.count as f32 / self.total as f32 * 100.0f32\n    }\n\n    /// C++: oss << frames_[i] << \" \" << std::fixed << std::setprecision(1)\n    ///          << percent << \"%\";  ==  printf(\"%s %.1f%%\", glyph, pct)\n    pub fn str(&self) -> String {\n        // cvtss2sd: widen the f32 result to f64 before formatting.\n        let p = self.percent_f32() as f64;\n        if p.is_nan() {\n            // Rust's {:.1} prints \"NaN\"; glibc/libstdc++ print \"-nan\"\n            // (x86 default QNaN from 0.0/0.0 is 0xFFC00000, sign bit set).\n            format!(\"{} -nan%\", self.frame())\n        } else {\n            // inf / -inf already match glibc exactly.\n            format!(\"{} {:.1}%\", self.frame(), p)\n        }\n    }\n}\n\nimpl std::fmt::Display for CyclicProgress {\n    fn fmt(&self, f: &mut std::fmt::Formatter<'_>) -> std::fmt::Result {\n        f.write_str(&self.str())\n    }\n}",
  "behaviors": [
    {
      "rule": "State is exactly two i32 fields plus a static 8-glyph frame table: count_ at object offset +0x00 (init 0), total_ at +0x04 (ctor arg), std::vector<std::string> frames_ at +0x08..+0x1F (sizeof object = 32).",
      "evidence": "Ctor 0x4038d0: `mov %esi,0x4(%rdi)` then `movl $0x0,(%rdi)`, followed by 8 calls to std::string::_S_construct. main allocates the three objects at 0x30/0x50/0x70(%rsp) — 32-byte stride. str() reads the vector as begin=0x8(%rbx), end=0x10(%rbx) with `sar $0x3` (8-byte COW std::string elements)."
    },
    {
      "rule": "next() is `count_ = (count_ + 1) % total_;` — signed C++ modulo, truncating toward zero. It does NOT simply increment and does NOT clamp.",
      "evidence": "main at 0x4022b4: `mov 0x30(%rsp),%eax; mov 0x34(%rsp),%ecx; add $0x1,%eax; cltd; idiv %ecx; lea 0x1(%rdx),%eax; cltd; idiv %ecx; ...; mov %edx,0x30(%rsp)`. The cyclic2/cyclic3 loops at 0x4022e0 and 0x402300 are the same `lea 0x1(%rdx),%eax; cltd; idiv %esi` body. No cmp/cmov/clamp instructions."
    },
    {
      "rule": "For total > 0, count_ is confined to [0, total-1], so the reported percentage is strictly less than 100.0% and 100.0% is unreachable through the public API. E.g. CyclicProgress(5) cycles counts 1,2,3,4,0,1,2,...",
      "evidence": "Harness /tmp/rev/edge2: `next cycle t=5: 1 2 3 4 0 1 2 3 4 0 1 2`. Max reachable string for total=5 is `⡿ 80.0%` (count 4)."
    },
    {
      "rule": "The spinner index is `count_ % frames_.size()` evaluated as size_t: the int is sign-extended to 64 bits and then divided UNSIGNED. For count_ = -1 the index is 7, not -1.",
      "evidence": "0x402bb4: `movslq (%rbx),%rax; xor %edx,%edx; div %rcx; mov (%rsi,%rdx,8),%rsi`. `div` (unsigned) after `movslq` (sign-extend). Confirmed: show(-1,8) -> `⣷ -12.5%` (idx 7); Rust `((-1i32) as i64 as u64) % 8 == 7`."
    },
    {
      "rule": "Frame table order and exact UTF-8 bytes: [0] E2A3BE U+28FE ⣾, [1] E2A3BD U+28FD ⣽, [2] E2A3BB U+28FB ⣻, [3] E2A2BF U+28BF ⢿, [4] E2A1BF U+287F ⡿, [5] E2A39F U+28DF ⣟, [6] E2A3AF U+28EF ⣯, [7] E2A3B7 U+28F7 ⣷. Index 3 is U+28BF; the supplied ground truth's U+283F is wrong.",
      "evidence": "`od -A x -c -j 0x4020` shows the 32-byte table at 0x404047 as 3-byte-glyph + NUL entries; ctor 0x4038d0 constructs strings from (0x404047,0x40404a), (0x40404b,0x40404e), (0x40404f,0x404052), (0x404053,0x404056), (0x404057,0x40405a), ... `grep -aob $'\\xe2\\xa0\\xbf'` (U+283F) finds nothing in the binary; `grep -aob $'\\xe2\\xa2\\xbf'` (U+28BF) hits offset 16467 = slot 3. Observed line 1 bytes 342 242 277 = E2 A2 BF."
    },
    {
      "rule": "str() composition is exactly: glyph, then one ASCII space (0x20), then the percentage in `std::fixed` with `std::setprecision(1)`, then one '%'. No leading/trailing padding, no newline. Equivalent to printf(\"%s %.1f%%\", glyph, pct).",
      "evidence": "Four __ostream_insert / _M_insert calls in order at 0x402bf3 (glyph.data(), glyph.size()), 0x402c08 (len 1 at 0x40403d = ' '), 0x402c51 (_M_insert<double>), 0x402c63 (len 1 at 0x404032 = '%'). od confirms [0x404032]='%' and [0x40403d]=' ' (the trailing space of the merged \"Progress: \" literal)."
    },
    {
      "rule": "The stream flags/precision are set inline, not via manipulator objects, and equal `std::fixed << std::setprecision(1)`: precision word := 1, floatfield cleared then fixed set.",
      "evidence": "0x402c2e `movq $0x1,0x8(%rdx)` (ios_base::_M_precision = 1) and 0x402c3a `and $0xfffffefb,%eax; or $0x4,%eax` — 0xfffffefb == ~0x104, where libstdc++ _S_fixed = 1<<2 = 0x4 and _S_scientific = 1<<8 = 0x100, so floatfield (0x104) is cleared and fixed is set."
    },
    {
      "rule": "The percentage is computed entirely in 32-bit float — `(float)count_ / (float)total_ * 100.0f` — and only widened to double for the stream insertion. Computing in f64 instead produces observably different, reachable output.",
      "evidence": "0x402c1b `cvtsi2ssl (%rbx),%xmm0`; 0x402c1f `cvtsi2ssl 0x4(%rbx),%xmm1`; 0x402c36 `divss %xmm1,%xmm0`; 0x402c45 `mulss 0x404068(%rip),%xmm0` where [0x404068] = 00 00 C8 42 = 100.0f (a 4-byte float, not a double); 0x402c4d `cvtss2sd`. Divergences found by scan: count=23,total=80 -> f32 \"28.8\" but f64 \"28.7\"; 47/80 -> \"58.7\" vs \"58.8\"; 49/80 -> \"61.2\" vs \"61.3\"; 51/80 -> \"63.8\" vs \"63.7\". All are reachable since count < total."
    },
    {
      "rule": "Rounding for the single decimal place is correct-rounding with round-half-to-even on exact binary ties, and Rust's `{:.1}` on the widened f64 matches glibc bit-for-bit on all tested finite values, including ties. No manual rounding helper is needed.",
      "evidence": "C++ (glibc): 6.25 -> \"6.2\", 18.75 -> \"18.8\", 31.25 -> \"31.2\", 43.75 -> \"43.8\", 3.125 -> \"3.1\", 9.375 -> \"9.4\". Rust `{:.1}` on the same f32-computed-then-widened values: 6.2, 18.8, 31.2, 43.8, 3.1, 9.4 — identical, and also identical on 23/80, 47/80, 49/80, 51/80, 3/5, 6/10, 9/15."
    },
    {
      "rule": "The ctor performs no validation of `total` (no zero check, no sign check, no clamp) and count_ always starts at 0.",
      "evidence": "Ctor 0x4038d0 body is only `mov %esi,0x4(%rdi)`, `movl $0x0,(%rdi)`, and the 8 string constructions — no test/cmp/branch on %esi."
    },
    {
      "rule": "main ignores argc/argv entirely; three CyclicProgress objects are constructed with 5, 10, 15, advanced 3/6/9 times, and printed with std::endl (LF, flushed).",
      "evidence": "main 0x402270: `mov $0x5,%esi` / `$0xa` / `$0xf` into the ctor calls; the three inlined next() loops; then str() + __ostream_insert to std::cout (0x406180) + endl. No reference to the incoming rdi/rsi (argc/argv) anywhere in main."
    }
  ],
  "edge_cases": [
    {
      "case": "total == 0, then next() is called",
      "decision": "Panic / abort. In Rust `self.count.wrapping_add(1) % self.total` panics with \"attempt to calculate the remainder with a divisor of zero\". Do not silently guard.",
      "rationale": "This is what the naive C++ does: `idiv` by zero raises SIGFPE. Verified: the harness with total=0 died with \"Floating point exception (core dumped)\", exit status 136. A guard would invent behavior the C++ does not have."
    },
    {
      "case": "total == 0, str() called without ever calling next() (count == 0)",
      "decision": "Return \"⣾ -nan%\" — i.e. glyph U+28FE, space, the literal \"-nan\", '%'. Special-case NaN, because Rust's `{:.1}` prints \"NaN\".",
      "rationale": "0.0f/0.0f on x86-64 SSE yields the hardware default QNaN 0xFFC00000, whose sign bit is set; glibc's printf therefore emits \"-nan\" (lowercase, with sign, and no \".0\" despite fixed/precision(1)). Verified twice: the real-str() harness printed `⣾ -nan%`, and an opaque/volatile-input probe printed `str=[-nan] bits=0xffc00000`. Rust prints \"NaN\" for any NaN sign, so an explicit branch is required for parity."
    },
    {
      "case": "total == 0 with a nonzero count forced into the object (count > 0)",
      "decision": "Return glyph + \" inf%\"; for count < 0 return glyph + \" -inf%\". No special-casing needed beyond NaN — Rust's `{:.1}` already prints \"inf\"/\"-inf\".",
      "rationale": "Verified through the real str() path: show(1,0) -> `⣽ inf%`, show(-1,0) -> `⣷ -inf%`. Rust `format!(\"{:.1}\", f64::INFINITY)` == \"inf\" and \"-inf\" for negative, matching glibc exactly. Note this state is unreachable via next() (which would have crashed first) — it only matters if the port exposes count as settable."
    },
    {
      "case": "count > total",
      "decision": "Do not clamp. str() reports the raw over-100 percentage, e.g. count=7,total=5 -> \"⣷ 140.0%\" and count=12,total=5 -> \"⡿ 240.0%\".",
      "rationale": "There is no clamping code in either next() (0x4022b8, no cmp/cmov) or str() (0x402ac0, straight divss/mulss). Verified against the real str() path. Note this state is UNREACHABLE through the public API because next() reduces mod total, so a Rust port that only exposes new()/next()/str() can never observe it; the no-clamp rule matters only for a from-parts constructor."
    },
    {
      "case": "Percentage reaching exactly 100.0%",
      "decision": "Treat as unreachable for total > 0; do not add a \"complete\" special case or a 100% terminal state.",
      "rationale": "next() wraps count to 0 at total, so count maxes out at total-1. CyclicProgress(5) tops out at \"⡿ 80.0%\" then returns to \"⣾ 0.0%\". Verified: `next cycle t=5: 1 2 3 4 0 ...`."
    },
    {
      "case": "negative total (e.g. -5)",
      "decision": "No special handling. next() yields the same non-negative cycle as |total| (1,2,3,4,0,...) and str() reports a negative percentage, e.g. count=3,total=-5 -> \"⢿ -60.0%\". Rust's `%` on i32 is truncating like C++'s, so the identical expression reproduces it.",
      "rationale": "C++ `%` truncates toward zero, so (count+1) % -5 == (count+1) % 5 for non-negative count+1. Verified: `next cycle t=-5: 1 2 3 4 0 1 2 3 4 0 1 2` — byte-identical to the total=5 cycle — and show(3,-5) -> `⢿ -60.0%`. A naive port gets this for free; adding abs() or a sign guard would diverge."
    },
    {
      "case": "total == 1",
      "decision": "count stays 0 forever; every str() returns \"⣾ 0.0%\" and the spinner never animates.",
      "rationale": "(0+1) % 1 == 0. Verified: `next cycle t=1: 0 0 0 0 0`. Worth a doc comment because it looks like a bug but faithfully mirrors the C++."
    },
    {
      "case": "integer overflow in next(): count == i32::MAX, or total == i32::MIN with count+1 == i32::MIN",
      "decision": "Use `wrapping_add(1)` for the increment (matches what the C++ actually does on x86 despite signed overflow being UB), and let the `%` panic on the i32::MIN / -1 overflow case.",
      "rationale": "`add $0x1,%eax` wraps silently; `idiv` raises SIGFPE on the INT_MIN / -1 quotient overflow, and Rust's `%` panics on exactly that pair. Plain `+ 1` in Rust would panic in debug builds on a case the C++ handles by wrapping, so wrapping_add is the faithful choice. Both cases require ~2 billion next() calls or a pathological total, so this is a correctness-of-record note, not a practical path."
    },
    {
      "case": "negative count reachable only by direct field mutation",
      "decision": "Index with ((count as i64) as u64) % 8, giving 7 for -1, 6 for -2, etc. — never a negative index.",
      "rationale": "The binary sign-extends with movslq then divides with unsigned `div`, which is precisely C++'s int -> size_t conversion in `count_ % frames_.size()`. Verified: show(-1,8) -> `⣷ ...` (index 7). A Rust port using `rem_euclid` would also give 7 here, but would differ for a hypothetical unsigned-wrap value; the cast chain is the exact transliteration."
    },
    {
      "case": "Non-ASCII width / terminal alignment of the glyph",
      "decision": "Emit the raw 3-byte UTF-8 sequence and treat the string as bytes; do not pad, normalize, or width-adjust.",
      "rationale": "str() inserts glyph.data() with glyph.size() == 3 and no padding fields set (only precision and floatfield are touched). Output is 3 glyph bytes + 1 space + digits + '%' — e.g. line 1 is exactly `342 242 277 40 36 30 56 60 45` (E2 A2 BF ' ' 6 0 . 0 %) per od -c."
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "High. Two independent lines of confirmation: (1) full disassembly of the ctor (0x4038d0), str() (0x402ac0), and the inlined next() in main (0x4022ac-0x40230b) pins every field offset, the modulo semantics, the f32 arithmetic, the precision/flag words, and each of the four inserted literals; (2) a reference C++ implementation built from that spec (/tmp/rev/ref.cpp, g++ -O2) produces output byte-identical to `test19_executable | head -3` (cmp: IDENTICAL) and compiles to an instruction-for-instruction matching str(). All edge cases in this report were executed, not inferred. Two corrections to the supplied ground truth are load-bearing and both are independently verified: index 3 of the charset is U+28BF (E2 A2 BF), not U+283F (whose UTF-8 is absent from the binary), and next() is `count = (count + 1) % total`, not a bare increment. The only residual uncertainty is cosmetic: the exact spelling of NaN output (\"-nan\") depends on x86-64's default QNaN sign and glibc's printf, which I verified on this machine but which is not guaranteed by the C++ standard; it is also unreachable via the public API since next() with total==0 crashes first."
}

