  "summary": "ProgressDisplay is an 8-byte POD holding exactly two i32 fields: `current` at offset 0 and `total` at offset 4 (proven by main's array stride of 8 at 0x4023b0-0x4023cc and by display() reading [rbx] / [rbx+4]). There is no other state. new(total) sets {current:0, total}; update(v) assigns current; increment(n) adds to current; total is never touched after construction. display() (out-of-line at 0x402ea0) builds a fresh std::ostringstream and emits exactly: \"Progress: \" + pct + \"% (\" + current + \"/\" + total + \")\" with no trailing newline and no clamping or branching whatsoever. CRITICAL CORRECTION to the supplied ground truth: the percentage is NOT current*100/total; the disassembly at 0x402f9c-0x402fb6 is cvtsi2ss(current), cvtsi2ss(total), divss, mulss 100.0f (constant 0x42C80000 at 0x404068) — i.e. `(current as f32 / total as f32) * 100.0f32`, division FIRST, all in single precision, then cvtss2sd to f64 for printing with fixed/precision(1). The two orders disagree on 2977 of the ~200M pairs with t<=20000 (e.g. 47/80 -> \"58.7\" for the binary's order vs \"58.8\" for current*100/total), so getting the order wrong is a real correctness bug. Verified bytes of lines 4-9 via od -c: \"Progress: 50.0% (25/50)\\n\", \"Progress: 75.0% (75/100)\\n\", \"Progress: 75.0% (150/200)\\n\", \"Progress: 60.0% (30/50)\\n\", \"Progress: 85.0% (85/100)\\n\", \"Progress: 85.0% (170/200)\\n\". I compiled an exact C++ replica with the same gcc-12 that built the binary; it reproduces those six lines byte-for-byte, then differential-tested my proposed Rust implementation against it over 422,104 (current,total) pairs (full sweep t in -3..400 x c in -5..420, plus 200k random pairs in +/-1e6, plus 50k full-range i32 pairs) -> cmp says IDENTICAL. Side note on the spinner ground truth (not this component): the 4th glyph in the binary's string table at 0x404047+ is U+28BF (bytes e2 a2 bf), not U+283F as stated; the rendered line 1 \"⢿\" confirms U+28BF.",
